course: dictionary
lesson: 10199
title: Words that tell you how something feels
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can name a feeling in your body, not just an object. «Tingle» is one of these. When a part of your body tingles, you feel lots of tiny prickles, like pins and needles.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. «Bang your elbow on a table and your little finger tingles» tells you the feeling comes from a knock, in one small part of the body. Swap in your meaning: "your little finger feels lots of tiny prickles" still makes sense, so you are right.
explain: Tingle can also be a feeling that runs through you. «A tingle ran down her spine» means a light, prickly shiver of excitement or fear. To tingle with excitement means you feel that excitement strongly all over.
fact: Tingle is a verb and a noun: you can tingle, or you can have a tingle.
fact: «Tingle» means to feel lots of tiny prickles, like pins and needles.
fact: «Tingle with excitement» means to feel excitement strongly all over.
fact: «A tingle down your spine» means a light, prickly shiver of fear or excitement.
fact: The funny bone is not a bone at all: it is a nerve lying close under the skin, which is why it tingles when you bang it.
exercise: «Bang your elbow on a table and your little finger tingles.» What does tingles mean here? | Stops moving and goes still | *Feels lots of tiny prickles | Turns bright red and hot | Swells up and gets bigger
exercise: «A tingle ran down her spine.» What feeling does this suggest? | Anger at her brother | Hunger after a long walk | *Excitement or fear | Tiredness
exercise: Find and copy the word in this sentence that names the light prickly feeling: «A fine powder filled the air and caressed the cheek with a tingle.» | caressed | filled | *tingle | powder
test: Sitting on a nerve stops its messages for a while, and «the tingle of pins and needles» is what? | *The nerve waking up | The nerve going dead for ever | A sign you have eaten too much | A bruise forming
test: Which phrase shows you feel excitement strongly all over? | *Tingle with excitement | Lost in the wood | Cold fingers | Fast asleep
exam: Jayden says a tingle is always a bad thing. Is he right? | Yes, a tingle always means something is wrong | *No, a tingle can be excitement, or a nerve waking up | No, a tingle only ever means you are cold | Yes, a tingle always means you are ill
